# Reverse-engineer and re-implement a command-line program

A working reference build of a command-line program is installed in this
environment, together with whatever documentation ships with it (try its
`--help`/`-h`, `man` pages, and observe how it behaves on real inputs). The
program's source code has been removed — you only have the black-box binary.

Your job is to re-implement the program from scratch so that your build
reproduces its observable behavior.
{% if target_language %}
You MUST implement the deliverable in {{ target_language }}. Write all source code in {{ target_language }}. Do not reimplement the program in another language.
{% endif %}
## What to deliver

Work inside `/workspace`. When you are done, that directory must contain:

- Your complete source code.
- A `compile.sh` script that builds your program.

`compile.sh` will be run from `/workspace` and **must** produce a single
executable at `/workspace/executable`.

{% if use_gradle %}
## Build with Gradle

You MUST structure the implementation as a Gradle project. `compile.sh` MUST invoke
Gradle to build the program.

Gradle {{ gradle_version }} is pre-installed on your `PATH`, and `GRADLE_USER_HOME`
is pre-seeded with the matching wrapper distribution cache.
{% endif %}

## How you are scored

After you finish, `/workspace/executable` is rebuilt from your `compile.sh`
and a hidden behavioral test suite runs it against many inputs,
comparing its output to the reference program. Your score is the
fraction of tests that pass. Aim to match the reference program's behavior as
closely as possible, including edge cases, flags, exit codes, and output
formatting.
